@meta
id: 06-03
title: The last character, not past it
chapter: 06
type: lesson
skills: line-end
par: 36
minutes: 4
vscode: End, then Left, then type
open: CHANGELOG.md

@brief
Release notes. Each bullet ends with a ticket reference in brackets, and the
team has decided the reference should also carry the owning function:

    (ORD-418, finance)
    (ORD-431, support)

Add those owners. The text goes inside the brackets, so the closing bracket
has to stay where it is and you have to land just short of the end of the
line rather than past it.

@keys
| `$` | the last character of the line |
| `i` | insert to the left of the cursor |
| `a` | insert to the right of the cursor |
| `A` | the same as `$` then `a` |

@hints
`$` takes you to the last character on the line however long the line is.
Whether you then press `i` or `a` decides which side of that character your
text lands on.
--
The last character is the closing bracket. You want your text before it, so
`i` rather than `a`.
--
`2j` to the first bullet, `$`, `i`, type `, finance`, `Esc`. Then `j`, `$`,
`i`, `, support`, `Esc`.

@solution
    2j $ i , finance <Esc> j $ i , support <Esc> :wq<Enter>

`A` — the key chapter 3 gave you — is precisely `$a`, and it is the right
choice when you are adding to the end of a line. It is the wrong choice
here, because the end of the line is already correct and the gap you want is
one character to its left.

That is the whole of `$`: it is a motion, so you get to choose what happens
when you arrive. Landing on the last character and pressing `i` is how you
add an argument inside a closing paren, a property inside a closing brace, a
word inside a closing quote. From chapter 8 onwards `$` also becomes the
target of an operator, and then it does far more than travel.

@start CHANGELOG.md
## 2024.3.2

- Refunds outside the 30 day window are now rejected (ORD-418)
- Cancelling a shipped order returns a 409 instead of a 500 (ORD-431)

